WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.244
Expectation is a probability-weighted average, not a promise about one trial

00:00:05.244 --> 00:00:13.170
Suppose the possible payoffs are zero, four, and ten with probabilities one half, three tenths, and one fifth

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:17.133
More likely values receive more weight in the average.

00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:22.526
Multiply each payoff by its probability, then add the contributions

00:00:22.526 --> 00:00:28.666
The result is three point two, even though that value is not a possible payoff

00:00:28.666 --> 00:00:36.033
For repeated independent trials of this model, sample averages settle near this expected value in the long run.

00:00:37.333 --> 00:00:42.075
Expectation respects addition and scaling when the relevant expectations exist

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:48.713
Doubling every payoff and adding one therefore doubles the old expectation and adds one

00:00:48.713 --> 00:00:56.300
This linearity does not require independence between quantities; even adding X to itself follows the rule.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:06.667
Paying three point two to play gives zero expected net payoff, but an individual outcome can still lose or gain money

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:16.167
Expected value summarizes an average; it does not describe variability, guarantee fairness in every trial, or eliminate the possibility of a loss.
